{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016PYkX4fgux9MtVaMSCU4tu","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→package dns\n     2→\n     3→import (\n     4→\t\"fmt\"\n     5→\t\"net\"\n     6→\t\"strings\"\n     7→\n     8→\tmdns \"github.com/miekg/dns\"\n     9→)\n    10→\n    11→// Server is an embedded DNS server that resolves *.test domains to 127.0.0.1.\n    12→type Server struct {\n    13→\tudpServer *mdns.Server\n    14→\ttcpServer *mdns.Server\n    15→}\n    16→\n    17→// handler responds to DNS queries.\n    18→type handler struct{}\n    19→\n    20→func (h *handler) ServeDNS(w mdns.ResponseWriter, r *mdns.Msg) {\n    21→\tmsg := new(mdns.Msg)\n    22→\tmsg.SetReply(r)\n    23→\tmsg.Authoritative = true\n    24→\n    25→\tfor _, q := range r.Question {\n    26→\t\tif q.Qtype == mdns.TypeA && strings.HasSuffix(strings.ToLower(q.Name), \".test.\") {\n    27→\t\t\tmsg.Answer = append(msg.Answer, &mdns.A{\n    28→\t\t\t\tHdr: mdns.RR_Header{\n    29→\t\t\t\t\tName:   q.Name,\n    30→\t\t\t\t\tRrtype: mdns.TypeA,\n    31→\t\t\t\t\tClass:  mdns.ClassINET,\n    32→\t\t\t\t\tTtl:    60,\n    33→\t\t\t\t},\n    34→\t\t\t\tA: net.ParseIP(\"127.0.0.1\"),\n    35→\t\t\t})\n    36→\t\t}\n    37→\t}\n    38→\n    39→\tif len(msg.Answer) == 0 {\n    40→\t\tmsg.Rcode = mdns.RcodeNameError\n    41→\t}\n    42→\n    43→\t_ = w.WriteMsg(msg)\n    44→}\n    45→\n    46→// New creates a DNS server that listens on the given address (e.g., \"127.0.0.1:53\").\n    47→func New(addr string) *Server {\n    48→\th := &handler{}\n    49→\n    50→\treturn &Server{\n    51→\t\tudpServer: &mdns.Server{Addr: addr, Net: \"udp\", Handler: h},\n    52→\t\ttcpServer: &mdns.Server{Addr: addr, Net: \"tcp\", Handler: h},\n    53→\t}\n    54→}\n    55→\n    56→// Start begins serving DNS on both UDP and TCP. It blocks until the servers are ready.\n    57→func (s *Server) Start() error {\n    58→\tudpReady := make(chan struct{})\n    59→\ttcpReady := make(chan struct{})\n    60→\terrCh := make(chan error, 2)\n    61→\n    62→\ts.udpServer.NotifyStartedFunc = func() { close(udpReady) }\n    63→\ts.tcpServer.NotifyStartedFunc = func() { close(tcpReady) }\n    64→\n    65→\tgo func() {\n    66→\t\tif err := s.udpServer.ListenAndServe(); err != nil {\n    67→\t\t\terrCh <- fmt.Errorf(\"udp dns server: %w\", err)\n    68→\t\t}\n    69→\t}()\n    70→\n    71→\tgo func() {\n    72→\t\tif err := s.tcpServer.ListenAndServe(); err != nil {\n    73→\t\t\terrCh <- fmt.Errorf(\"tcp dns server: %w\", err)\n    74→\t\t}\n    75→\t}()\n    76→\n    77→\t// Wait for both servers to be ready, or for an error.\n    78→\tfor i := 0; i < 2; i++ {\n    79→\t\tselect {\n    80→\t\tcase <-udpReady:\n    81→\t\t\tudpReady = nil\n    82→\t\tcase <-tcpReady:\n    83→\t\t\ttcpReady = nil\n    84→\t\tcase err := <-errCh:\n    85→\t\t\treturn err\n    86→\t\t}\n    87→\t}\n    88→\n    89→\treturn nil\n    90→}\n    91→\n    92→// Stop gracefully shuts down both UDP and TCP servers.\n    93→func (s *Server) Stop() error {\n    94→\tvar firstErr error\n    95→\tif err := s.udpServer.Shutdown(); err != nil && firstErr == nil {\n    96→\t\tfirstErr = err\n    97→\t}\n    98→\tif err := s.tcpServer.Shutdown(); err != nil && firstErr == nil {\n    99→\t\tfirstErr = err\n   100→\t}\n   101→\treturn firstErr\n   102→}\n   103→"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3e2ec84-c6d0-495a-acfd-c629e33a07ba","uuid":"6934c90b-06bf-49b2-ac83-be5a9c2f3fd8","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/vanderlei/vans/wtm/internal/dns/dns.go","content":"package dns\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"net\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\tmdns \"github.com/miekg/dns\"\n)\n\n// Server is an embedded DNS server that resolves *.test domains to 127.0.0.1.\ntype Server struct {\n\tudpServer *mdns.Server\n\ttcpServer *mdns.Server\n}\n\n// handler responds to DNS queries.\ntype handler struct{}\n\nfunc (h *handler) ServeDNS(w mdns.ResponseWriter, r *mdns.Msg) {\n\tmsg := new(mdns.Msg)\n\tmsg.SetReply(r)\n\tmsg.Authoritative = true\n\n\tfor _, q := range r.Question {\n\t\tif q.Qtype == mdns.TypeA && strings.HasSuffix(strings.ToLower(q.Name), \".test.\") {\n\t\t\tmsg.Answer = append(msg.Answer, &mdns.A{\n\t\t\t\tHdr: mdns.RR_Header{\n\t\t\t\t\tName:   q.Name,\n\t\t\t\t\tRrtype: mdns.TypeA,\n\t\t\t\t\tClass:  mdns.ClassINET,\n\t\t\t\t\tTtl:    60,\n\t\t\t\t},\n\t\t\t\tA: net.ParseIP(\"127.0.0.1\"),\n\t\t\t})\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\tif len(msg.Answer) == 0 {\n\t\tmsg.Rcode = mdns.RcodeNameError\n\t}\n\n\t_ = w.WriteMsg(msg)\n}\n\n// New creates a DNS server that listens on the given address (e.g., \"127.0.0.1:53\").\nfunc New(addr string) *Server {\n\th := &handler{}\n\n\treturn &Server{\n\t\tudpServer: &mdns.Server{Addr: addr, Net: \"udp\", Handler: h},\n\t\ttcpServer: &mdns.Server{Addr: addr, Net: \"tcp\", Handler: h},\n\t}\n}\n\n// Start begins serving DNS on both UDP and TCP. It blocks until the servers are ready.\nfunc (s *Server) Start() error {\n\tudpReady := make(chan struct{})\n\ttcpReady := make(chan struct{})\n\terrCh := make(chan error, 2)\n\n\ts.udpServer.NotifyStartedFunc = func() { close(udpReady) }\n\ts.tcpServer.NotifyStartedFunc = func() { close(tcpReady) }\n\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tif err := s.udpServer.ListenAndServe(); err != nil {\n\t\t\terrCh <- fmt.Errorf(\"udp dns server: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t}()\n\n\tgo func() {\n\t\tif err := s.tcpServer.ListenAndServe(); err != nil {\n\t\t\terrCh <- fmt.Errorf(\"tcp dns server: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t}()\n\n\t// Wait for both servers to be ready, or for an error.\n\tfor i := 0; i < 2; i++ {\n\t\tselect {\n\t\tcase <-udpReady:\n\t\t\tudpReady = nil\n\t\tcase <-tcpReady:\n\t\t\ttcpReady = nil\n\t\tcase err := <-errCh:\n\t\t\treturn err\n\t\t}\n\t}\n\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// Stop gracefully shuts down both UDP and TCP servers.\nfunc (s *Server) Stop() error {\n\tvar firstErr error\n\tif err := s.udpServer.Shutdown(); err != nil && firstErr == nil {\n\t\tfirstErr = err\n\t}\n\tif err := s.tcpServer.Shutdown(); err != nil && firstErr == nil {\n\t\tfirstErr = err\n\t}\n\treturn firstErr\n}\n","numLines":103,"startLine":1,"totalLines":103}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01X4D7YPn6CgSszMZX8dDzwj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand the architecture. The PRD calls for using embedded Caddy (`github.com/caddyserver/caddy/v2`) with `caddy.Load()` to programmatically configure the reverse proxy.\n\nHowever, embedding Caddy as a library is extremely heavy (it pulls in hundreds of dependencies and significantly increases binary size). Also, Caddy v2's programmatic API uses JSON configuration.\n\nLet me think about what approach to take. The acceptance criteria says:\n1. Reverse proxy listens on ports 80 and 443\n2. Each registered site gets tls internal and reverse_proxy to its upstream\n3. Caddy config is built programmatically via caddy.Load() — no Caddyfile on disk\n\n\n4. Reload(table) rebuilds and hot-reloads config from the current routing table\n5. Integration test: start proxy on high ports, register a route, verify HTTPS request reaches a test HTTP server\n\nI need to commit to using Caddy as a library despite the overhead. The JSON config format is well-documented, so I'll start building the `internal/proxy` package with the core function that constructs the Caddy configuration from the routing table.\n\nMy approach: add the Caddy dependency, create a Server struct that wraps Caddy, implement Start() and Reload() methods, and write a helper to generate the JSON config. I'll also need a Stop() method and an integration test that spins up the proxy on high ports and verifies HTTPS requests work end-to-end.\n\nBuilding the Caddy JSON config structure... I'm thinking through which Caddy modules need to be imported for this setup—the core library, HTTP app, reverse proxy handler, and TLS components—though when using Caddy's JSON API with `caddy.Load()`, the framework handles module loading automatically as long as the modules are available in the build.\n\nActually, I realize the cleaner approach is to build the JSON config as a map and marshal it, then pass it to `caddy.Load()`, which sidesteps needing to import all the typed structs directly. But Caddy modules still need to be registered via blank imports, so I'll use the standard pattern of importing the `standard` module package which registers all the common modules like HTTP, TLS, and reverse proxy in one go.\n\nNow for the integration test itself, I need to spin up a test HTTP server on a random port, configure a routing table that points a domain to that server, and then start Caddy with that configuration.\n\nFor the HTTPS request part of the test, I'll need to either configure a custom TLS client that trusts Caddy's internal CA or disable TLS verification since we're in a test environment. I should also check whether Caddy v2 is already in the go.mod—if not, I'll need to add it as a dependency. The standard modules import brings in a lot of dependencies, but it's the recommended approach.\n\nWriting test code...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3959,"cache_read_input_tokens":28103,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3959},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3e2ec84-c6d0-495a-acfd-c629e33a07ba","uuid":"a75aff08-cbd6-4be7-a221-906ec634ea94"}
