{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01MQsfmjf6d7GfAmroiBzBsk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The issue is a TLS internal error. Looking at the logs, the first test shows:\n```\n\"cert lifetime would exceed issuer NotAfter, clamping lifetime\",\"orig_lifetime\":43200,\"lifetime\":-1412216.549055,\"not_after\":1771346981\n```\n\nThis suggests the internal CA's root certificate has expired (the not_after is in the past). The negative lifetime is a problem.\n\nActually, looking more carefully, the log says `\"cert lifetime would exceed issuer NotAfter, clamping lifetime\"` with a negative lifetime. This means the internal CA root cert has expired. The cert was previously generated and stored. I need to deal with this.\n\n\nBut the tests do succeed in getting the cert, so the real problem is that the certificate lifetime was clamped to a negative value, making it immediately expired and causing the TLS handshake to fail on the client side.\n\nLooking at the first test output, I can see the cert lifetime calculation shows a negative value, which means the generated certificate would already be expired. The root CA itself has expired, so I need to regenerate it by clearing out the old CA files from Caddy's storage.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3314,"cache_read_input_tokens":39579,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3314},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b3e2ec84-c6d0-495a-acfd-c629e33a07ba","uuid":"6d1c3f10-af1d-42c8-bd53-06aecf725147"}
