{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01FFbK3tjnGjMe95j1f73CK1","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The certificate was renewed successfully and the cache shows `new_expiration:1772802423` which is in the future. But still getting \"tls: internal error\". The issue might be that the certificate was obtained but there's an issue with the TLS handshake. Looking at the logs, I see \"stapling OCSP\" warnings - since internal CA doesn't have OCSP, this could be causing issues.\n\nLet me look at this differently. The \"tls: internal error\" from the server side usually means the server encountered an error during TLS handshake. Let me check if it's related to OCSP stapling or something else.\n\nActually, I think the issue might be that when Caddy serves on a high port, the auto-HTTPS redirect server also tries to bind to a different port. \n\nI'm wondering if the TLS connection policies need different configuration, or if there's something wrong with Caddy's internal TLS setup right after provisioning the certificate. Maybe disabling OCSP stapling or switching to `on_demand` TLS would help. The \"tls: internal error\" alert could mean Caddy can't locate the certificate for the requested SNI, even though the host is set correctly.\n\nWait—I think I see the real issue. When making an HTTPS request to `https://127.0.0.1:53484/`, the TLS SNI comes from the URL hostname (127.0.0.1), not the Host header. So Caddy is trying to find a certificate for 127.0.0.1 but only has one for myapp.test. I need to set the ServerName in the transport config or connect directly to myapp.test instead.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2857,"cache_read_input_tokens":43410,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2857},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management